/** @page about Mosaic
  @section purpose What Mosaic is

  A brokerless, verifiable, replicated-log library and daemon for C++20 on ZeroMQ. This documentation describes version 0.6.0. Every writer owns signed,
  hash-chained, append-only logs per channel; nodes subscribe to channel patterns, replicate the logs they follow
  directly from each other, and repair anything they missed from any peer that holds it. There is no broker, no
  consensus and no coordinator in the data path.

  @section where Where the truth lives

  The behaviour of the system is specified in `openspec/specs/` - one capability per file, requirements and
  scenarios. The reasoning behind those specifications is in `docs/design/`, starting with `00-overview.md`, and
  the work items are on the board in `docs/design/TODO.md`.

  @section borrowed What came from where

  Mosaic is not a fork. It borrows two designs and rejects a third, and it is worth being precise about which is which:

  - **From [Dafka](https://github.com/zeromq/dafka) ([RFC 46](https://rfc.zeromq.org/spec/46)):** the shape. One writer owns an
    append-only log per channel; the log is single-writer, so replicating it needs no consensus; replicas are passive and consumers
    own their position; the message set is RECORD / HEAD / FETCH / ACK. Mosaic keeps the shape and none of the code - Dafka is C,
    built on LevelDB and czmq draft APIs, and this library is C++20 with SQLite or memory behind a seam.
  - **From [Malamute](https://github.com/zeromq/malamute):** the ideas that were described but never implemented. Credit-based flow
    control, where the receiver paces the sender and nobody blocks; subject patterns with matching decided locally; and the service
    pattern, which Mosaic expresses as records on `svc.<name>` rather than as a broker operation.
  - **Rejected:** Malamute's broker, and Dafka's tower. Both are central points; this project has none in the data path.
  - **Not taken from either:** signed hash-chained records, durable writer identity (the public key, not a session id), retention
    governed by replica acknowledgements, and repair served by any holder rather than the writer. Those are Mosaic's own, and each
    is a written requirement rather than an aspiration.

  Nothing on this site is copied from those projects: their text is MPL-2.0, so their *structure* is borrowed and credited while
  the prose here is written from Mosaic's own capability specs.

  @section modulemap The modules

  - core: records, hashing, signing seams, keystore
  - store: the persistence seam (memory, SQLite) and fork evidence
  - log: writer-side chaining, replica-side verification, static store verification
  - proto: the binary wire format and its total decoder
  - net: the transport contract, the ZeroMQ transport, peer pinning, deterministic simulation
  - node: the single-threaded state machine wiring everything together
  - flow: credit control
  - match: channel pattern matching
  - jobs: calling a service by name over logs
*/

/* These two blocks are not decoration: m.css only indexes a compound that has a brief or a description, and it nests members
   under their parent. An undocumented `mosaic` namespace therefore fails that test and is dropped - taking every class and
   struct inside it out of the Classes and Namespaces indexes with it. Documenting the namespace is what makes the site
   navigable. See the change `repair-and-brand-the-reference-site` and scripts/check_site.sh, which fails if the indexes come
   back empty. */

/** @namespace mosaic
  @brief The library's public interface.

  Records and their hashing and signing seams, the store and log layers, channel matching, credit control, the transports, and
  the node that wires them together. A caller that includes `<mosaic/...>` headers is talking to this namespace.
*/

/** @namespace mosaic::daemon
  @brief Configuration and the long-running process built from it.

  ZPL parsing with whole-file validation, the printable defaults, and the `mosaicd` run loop that owns time and the store.
*/
